course: world
lesson: 106
title: Late and early: being where you need to be on time
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Being on time is a sum done backwards. Ollie wakes at 8:20; school starts at 8:45 and the walk takes 20 minutes. Count back from the start: 8:45 take away 20 minutes is 8:25, the latest he can leave. So he has just 5 minutes to get ready. "Bloody hell, I'm dead," he mutters, meaning he's in big trouble.
explain: Important trips need a buffer, spare time in case something goes wrong. Bianca's family planned to leave for the airport at 9:00 but left at 9:25 because Mum lost her passport. The drive is 50 minutes, so they arrive at 10:15, and check-in closes at 10:30: the buffer saved them, with 15 minutes to spare.
explain: "On time" means different things in different places. For school, trains, the dentist and a match, be on time or a bit early; a coach who says "if you're on time, you're late" wants you there early to warm up. For a birthday party many people come a little after the start, so arriving at 3:00 sharp can mean sitting alone with the birthday boy's mum.
explain: People also joke about time. "Glad you could join us" said to someone 10 minutes late is sarcasm: kind words that really mean "you're late and I noticed". "Two secs" and "Irish time" are not real times at all.
explain: Small lateness adds up: 10 minutes late every school day for about 190 days is 1,900 minutes, more than 31 hours of school missed.
fact: To find when to leave, take the journey time away from the time you must arrive: 8:45 − 20 minutes = 8:25.
fact: A buffer is extra time kept spare in case something goes wrong, such as a lost passport or traffic.
fact: Leaving at 9:25 with a 50-minute drive means arriving at 10:15, which is 15 minutes before a 10:30 check-in closes.
fact: Some coaches and teachers want you there before the start, so you can warm up or settle in; "on time" for them means early.
fact: At parties in many countries people arrive a little after the start time, but for school, trains and appointments you should be on time.
fact: Sarcasm means saying the opposite of what you mean to make a point, like "glad you could join us" to someone who is late.
fact: Arriving 5 to 15 minutes early for an appointment is good: you can sit down, calm down and fill in forms.
fact: "Irish time" and "island time" are jokes about being relaxed about lateness at casual events; they do not count for school, flights or work.
fact: "Two secs", "one sec" and "in a minute" are not real times; they mean soon, and soon can be 10 minutes, which is 600 seconds.
fact: If you will be late, it is polite to tell people early, for example with a text saying "running late".
fact: A school marks the register at a set time; arriving at 9:05 for an 8:55 register is 10 minutes late.
fact: 10 minutes late on about 190 school days adds up to 1,900 minutes, which is more than 31 hours.
fact: Words people use: "I'm dead" here means "I'm in big trouble", "sharp" means exactly on time, "keen" means eager, "my backside!" means "I don't believe you" (rude-ish, UK), "grand" means fine (Ireland) and "tamping" means furious (Wales).
exercise: School starts at 8:45 and Ollie's walk takes 20 minutes. What is the latest he can leave? | 8:25 | 8:05 | 8:40 | 8:20
exercise: Ollie wakes at 8:20 and must leave at 8:25. How long has he got to get ready? | 5 minutes | 25 minutes | 20 minutes | 45 minutes
exercise: Lily arrives at the party at 3:00 sharp. Others come at 3:20. How much earlier than them is she? | 20 minutes | 3 minutes | 10 minutes | 30 minutes
exercise: Training starts at 10:00 but the coach wants players at 9:45. How "late" is someone who comes at 10:00? | 15 minutes | No time at all | 45 minutes | 10 minutes
exercise: Bianca's family leaves at 9:25 and the drive is 50 minutes. When do they arrive? | 10:15 | 10:25 | 9:75 | 10:05
exercise: They arrive at 10:15 and check-in closes at 10:30. How much time is spare? | 15 minutes | 25 minutes | 5 minutes | No time at all
exercise: What is a buffer when you travel? | Extra spare time in case something goes wrong | A snack you take for the journey | The quickest road to the airport | A ticket that lets you be late
exercise: Class starts at 9:00 and Jacob walks in at 9:10. How late is he? | 10 minutes | 9 minutes | 1 minute | 19 minutes
exercise: The teacher says, "Morning, glad you could join us," to late Jacob. What does she really mean? | You're late, and I noticed | I'm really happy you're here early | You can be late any time you like | Please go home and come back tomorrow
exercise: Freya's family says "about six" and turns up at 6:40. How late are they? | 40 minutes | 6 minutes | 4 minutes | 1 hour 40 minutes
exercise: Malik's appointment is at 4:10. He arrives at 3:55. How early is he? | 15 minutes | 25 minutes | 5 minutes | 55 minutes
exercise: Jess's uncle is 20 minutes late to the barbecue and says, "We're on Irish time." What is he doing? | Joking about being relaxed about lateness | Telling them Ireland is 20 minutes ahead | Saying his watch is set to Irish time | Explaining that barbecues start late by law
exercise: Toby says "two secs" and takes 10 minutes. How many seconds is 10 minutes? | 600 | 100 | 1,000 | 120
exercise: The register is at 8:55. Lucy arrives at 9:05. How late is she? | 10 minutes | 5 minutes | 15 minutes | 1 minute
exercise: Being 10 minutes late on about 190 school days adds up to how many minutes? | 1,900 | 190 | 19,000 | 200
exercise: For which of these is it fine to arrive a few minutes after the start? | A friend's birthday party | A train leaving the station | A dentist appointment | A school exam
test: Rory: "Mam says if you're on time you're late. That makes no sense!" What does she mean? | Arrive a bit early, so you're ready when it starts | Always arrive after the start time | Clocks in Ireland run late | Being on time is rude
test: Ollie wakes at 8:20, school at 8:45, a 20-minute walk. He says, "Sweet, I've got 25 minutes." What did he forget? | The 20-minute walk; he has only 5 minutes | Nothing, he has 25 minutes | Breakfast takes 25 minutes | School starts at 9:00 really
test: What would happen if Bianca's family had planned no buffer and left at 9:40 instead of 9:25, with the same 50-minute drive? | They would arrive at 10:30 and only just make check-in | They would arrive at 10:15 with time to spare | They would arrive at 10:00, very early | They would arrive at 11:00 and miss it
test: Freya's gran, proper tamping: "Roast was ready at six. You rolled in at twenty to seven!" How late were they? | 40 minutes | 20 minutes | 1 hour 20 minutes | 7 minutes
test: The dentist's receptionist says "Ooh, keen!" when Malik arrives 15 minutes early. What does she mean? | He's eager, and early is fine | He's in trouble for arriving early | He must leave and come back later | He has missed his appointment
test: Toby: "Ready in two secs!" Ten minutes later his sister yells, "Two secs my backside, I'm going without you!" What's her point? | Two secs was a promise he didn't keep | Two seconds really is ten minutes | She wants him to take longer | She thinks seconds are longer than minutes
test: Lucy, salty (annoyed): "It's only 10 minutes, who cares?" If she's 10 minutes late every school day for 190 days, how much does she miss? | More than 31 hours | About 3 hours | Exactly 10 hours | About 19 minutes
test: Jacob grins when the teacher says "glad you could join us", thinking she's pleased. What did he miss? | It was sarcasm meaning "you're late" | It was a real thank-you | It meant he was early | It was a joke about the weather
test: A cousin from Dublin says, "Grand, we'll come on Irish time." Should you use Irish time for a flight? | No, flights leave on time without you | Yes, planes wait 20 minutes for Irish people | Yes, as long as you text the pilot | No, flights always leave early
test: Lily, 3:00 sharp at a party, sits alone with the birthday boy's mum till 3:20. Did she do anything wrong? | No, she was on time; party guests often come a bit later | Yes, she should have come at 2:40 | Yes, parties always start an hour late | No, the others came 20 minutes early
test: In a cartoon a rabbit runs in shouting, "I'm late!" but checks no clock and never counts back. What should he do? | Count back from when he must arrive to find when to leave | Run faster without looking at any clock | Arrive late on purpose every time | Set his watch 20 minutes slow
exam: It is given that school commences at 8:45 and that the journey thereto requires twenty minutes. Determine the latest permissible time of departure. | 8:25 | 8:05 | 8:40 | 9:05
exam: Whereas a party departs at 9:25 upon a drive of fifty minutes, and check-in closes at 10:30, ascertain the time remaining upon arrival. | Fifteen minutes | Twenty-five minutes | Five minutes | No time whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the remark "Glad you could join us" when addressed to a pupil arriving late. | It is sarcasm signifying disapproval of the lateness | It is sincere gratitude for punctual attendance | It is an invitation to depart forthwith | It is a commendation for arriving early
exam: Ascertain the aggregate time lost by a pupil arriving ten minutes late upon each of one hundred and ninety school days. | One thousand nine hundred minutes | One hundred and ninety minutes | Nineteen thousand minutes | Two hundred minutes
